<Base
  title="About — Macadish"
  description="Why I measure dough, what a home kitchen can and cannot measure, and the one axis most pizza decisions sit on."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <p class="label">Draft · Singapore</p>
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      I make pizza in a home kitchen in Singapore, across four doughs, and I write
      down what happens. This page is why.
    </p>
  </div>

  <article class="essay">
    <p class="standfirst">
      Most pizza advice is a description of one person's kitchen, generalised into a
      rule. I would like to know which parts actually transfer.
    </p>

    <h2>Many roads, same crust</h2>

    <p>
      Cold ferment against long ferment against same-day. Bulk before balling, or
      balling before the cold. Poolish, biga, direct. One flour or a blend.
      Sixty-two per cent hydration or ninety. People reach a light, open crust from
      wildly different starting points, and each of them will tell you their route
      is the one that matters.
    </p>

    <p>
      They cannot all be right, and I do not think they are all wrong either. What
      I think it means is that the variables doing the real work are not the ones
      being argued about — they are the ones nobody can measure at home.
    </p>

    <h2>What a kitchen can and cannot see</h2>

    <p>
      Doubling in volume. The windowpane. A pH strip. How the preferment smells.
      These are proxies, and they work — but they work for <em>your</em> dough,
      as a way of hitting the same result twice. They are not precise enough to
      compare two doughs built from different inputs, which is exactly what you
      need if you want to know why something worked.
    </p>

    <p>
      What I would actually want is a single probe reading pH and acid composition,
      temperature, yeast activity and population, remaining sugar, volatile
      compounds, and rheology. Give bakers the instruments scientists have and a lot
      of confident advice would quietly disappear.
    </p>

    <h2>The axis everything sits on</h2>

    <p>
      Extensible enough to open, strong enough to spring. Almost every choice I make
      is really just picking a point between those two: poolish, warmth and water
      buy extensibility; biga, cooler handling and stronger flour buy structure and
      a puffy rim. Naming the axis makes the decisions legible — most of the time I
      am not choosing a method, I am choosing where on that line to sit.
    </p>

    <h2>Twenty-nine degrees</h2>

    <p>
      A Singapore kitchen sits around 29 °C, which is warmer than most recipes
      assume and warm enough to run away from you. So I ferment in a cooler box with
      an ice pack — not to hold the dough cold, but as a slow warming ramp: down to
      about 9 °C for an hour, then climbing to 20 °C over seven, capping well below
      the room.
    </p>

    <p>
      Nothing in a European recipe accounts for that. Half of what I have learned is
      just working out which numbers travel and which ones were always local.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the failures are written down</h2>

    <p>
      A dough that works tells you that you are inside the window. A dough that
      fails tells you where the edge is, which is the more useful fact and the more
      expensive one to buy. So the over-proofed ones, the ones that tore on opening,
      the experiment that came back inconclusive because the mixer would not autolyse
      gently — those get an entry too.
    </p>

    <p>
      Every specification here carries two halves, and they matter equally: a number
      I can repeat, and a judgement I can only make with my hands in the dough.
      Anyone offering one without the other is leaving out the difficult part.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <p class="note">
      Placeholder draft, written from my working notes. To be rewritten in my own
      words before this goes anywhere.
    </p>
  </article>
